WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.843
Imagine a Torrance hearing where two maps appear to disagree.

2
00:00:03.843 --> 00:00:06.238
One colors a tract as highly burdened.

3
00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:09.388
Another shows a corridor as relatively advantaged.

4
00:00:09.388 --> 00:00:18.335
A speaker cites a percentage from the American Community Survey, but the number came from a different five-year release than the staff report.

5
00:00:18.335 --> 00:00:22.052
A map looks authoritative because its boundaries are crisp.

6
00:00:22.052 --> 00:00:30.306
Good planning begins by asking what the map measures, when it was measured, how uncertain it is, and what disappears at that scale.

7
00:00:30.306 --> 00:00:36.252
This episode is preparation, not legal, statistical, or survey advice for a specific decision.

8
00:00:36.252 --> 00:00:40.744
Datasets, geographic boundaries, web tools, and legal standards change.

9
00:00:40.744 --> 00:00:49.601
Use current metadata, official releases, local verification, the complete administrative record, and advice from the Torrance city attorney.

10
00:00:49.601 --> 00:00:52.068
Read every map from the outside inward.

11
00:00:52.068 --> 00:00:53.902
Start with title and purpose.

12
00:00:53.902 --> 00:00:57.951
Then read the source, date, geography, legend, units, and notes.

13
00:00:57.951 --> 00:01:04.657
Identify whether the values are counts, percentages, rates, percentiles, modeled estimates, or categories.

14
00:01:04.657 --> 00:01:06.492
Only then inspect the colors.

15
00:01:06.492 --> 00:01:08.073
A count answers how many.

16
00:01:08.073 --> 00:01:11.679
A percentage answers what share of a defined denominator.

17
00:01:11.679 --> 00:01:15.791
A rate usually normalizes by population, time, area, or exposure.

18
00:01:15.791 --> 00:01:19.903
A percentile ranks one place against others in the reference set.

19
00:01:19.903 --> 00:01:24.079
A modeled value estimates a condition from assumptions and inputs.

20
00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:25.976
These are not interchangeable.

21
00:01:25.976 --> 00:01:29.076
Color choices can exaggerate or hide differences.

22
00:01:29.076 --> 00:01:35.213
A classification method may use equal intervals, quantiles, natural breaks, or policy thresholds.

23
00:01:35.213 --> 00:01:39.578
The same data can produce different visual stories under each method.

24
00:01:39.578 --> 00:01:41.159
Ask for the break values.

25
00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:48.751
If two places on opposite sides of a break have nearly identical values, do not narrate them as fundamentally different.

26
00:01:48.751 --> 00:01:50.707
Geography creates another trap.

27
00:01:50.707 --> 00:02:01.182
Census tracts, block groups, blocks, ZIP Code Tabulation Areas, council districts, neighborhoods, parcels, and transportation analysis zones serve different purposes.

28
00:02:01.182 --> 00:02:05.095
Aggregating points into larger areas can hide local variation.

29
00:02:05.095 --> 00:02:09.197
Changing boundaries or scale can change an apparent relationship.

30
00:02:09.197 --> 00:02:12.920
This is sometimes called the modifiable areal unit problem.

31
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.750
Avoid the ecological fallacy.

32
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:20.871
If a tract has a high share of renters, that does not mean every person in the tract is a renter.

33
00:02:20.871 --> 00:02:25.982
Area-level data describes an area, not the identity or behavior of each resident.

34
00:02:25.982 --> 00:02:31.977
Do not infer an individual's income, race, travel pattern, or vulnerability from a map polygon.

35
00:02:31.977 --> 00:02:40.054
The decennial census aims to count the population and provides core demographic and housing information at detailed geographies.

36
00:02:40.054 --> 00:02:47.501
The American Community Survey, or ACS, provides a broader set of social, economic, housing, and demographic estimates.

37
00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:52.360
One-year ACS products cover larger populations with more current information.

38
00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:59.553
Five-year products combine more survey responses and reach smaller geographies, but represent a multi-year period.

39
00:02:59.553 --> 00:03:01.320
An ACS value is an estimate.

40
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.898
Read its margin of error.

41
00:03:02.898 --> 00:03:08.136
A difference between two estimates may not be meaningful when uncertainty is large.

42
00:03:08.136 --> 00:03:11.733
For a percentage, inspect both numerator and denominator.

43
00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:14.572
Small denominators can create unstable rates.

44
00:03:14.572 --> 00:03:24.858
When comparing releases, use compatible tables, geographies, inflation treatment where relevant, and nonoverlapping periods when trend claims require independence.

45
00:03:24.858 --> 00:03:27.761
Do not say the ACS interviewed every resident.

46
00:03:27.761 --> 00:03:31.863
Do not call a five-year estimate a measurement of the final year.

47
00:03:31.863 --> 00:03:38.426
Do not compare a city one-year estimate with a tract five-year estimate without explaining the mismatch.

48
00:03:38.426 --> 00:03:44.673
Use the Census Bureau's table identifiers and metadata so another analyst can reproduce the result.

49
00:03:44.673 --> 00:03:48.830
SCAG's public Toolbox Tuesday sessions support this data literacy.

50
00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:57.713
Its August 2025 session, Working With Census Data in R: Analyzing and Visualizing Trends, demonstrated reproducible access and visualization.

51
00:03:57.713 --> 00:04:04.957
Its May 2026 session on economic statistics explained major Census Bureau business datasets and newer access tools.

52
00:04:04.957 --> 00:04:09.115
Earlier SCAG sessions covered GIS modeling and regional analytics.

53
00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:14.280
The important habit is preserving a chain from source to transformation to visual.

54
00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:21.777
For every number, record the dataset, table or field, vintage, universe, geography, retrieval date, and transformation.

55
00:04:21.777 --> 00:04:25.305
The universe is the population eligible for the measure.

56
00:04:25.305 --> 00:04:28.895
A table about workers is not a table about all residents.

57
00:04:28.895 --> 00:04:34.880
A poverty measure may exclude or treat some populations differently under the table definition.

58
00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.077
Now add Greenprint.

59
00:04:36.077 --> 00:04:48.676
SCAG describes the SoCal Greenprint as an optional, flexible conservation-focused web-mapping tool for land-use and transportation decisions, conservation investment, funding, and mitigation planning.

60
00:04:48.676 --> 00:05:02.913
Its public thematic layers include agriculture and working lands, habitat and biodiversity, water resources, built environment, environmental justice and inclusion, climate vulnerability and resilience, and geographic context.

61
00:05:02.913 --> 00:05:06.692
Greenprint can bring multiple regional layers into one view.

62
00:05:06.692 --> 00:05:12.047
It can help a planner identify overlap, generate questions, and compare alternatives.

63
00:05:12.047 --> 00:05:13.937
It does not make the decision.

64
00:05:13.937 --> 00:05:19.669
A layer may have a different date, resolution, extent, or method from the layer beneath it.

65
00:05:19.669 --> 00:05:28.426
Overlap does not prove causation, ownership, legal jurisdiction, habitat condition on a parcel, or the presence of a regulatory constraint.

66
00:05:28.426 --> 00:05:33.402
SCAG's September 2026 public Toolbox Tuesday demonstrated the SoCal Greenprint.

67
00:05:33.402 --> 00:05:37.497
Use the tool according to current access terms and documentation.

68
00:05:37.497 --> 00:05:41.466
Cite the original layer when possible, not only the web viewer.

69
00:05:41.466 --> 00:05:46.505
If data is licensed, restricted, or proprietary, do not copy or redistribute it.

70
00:05:46.505 --> 00:05:54.002
The assignment in this course uses only public metadata, public outputs permitted by the source, and original analysis.

71
00:05:54.002 --> 00:06:02.053
For practical application, build a map-reading card for one public Torrance planning question, such as heat and transit access.

72
00:06:02.053 --> 00:06:06.427
Select one Census or ACS variable and one public environmental layer.

73
00:06:06.427 --> 00:06:09.661
Write the decision question before opening the map.

74
00:06:09.661 --> 00:06:12.323
Record the source details for both layers.

75
00:06:12.323 --> 00:06:13.908
Then complete six checks.

76
00:06:13.908 --> 00:06:17.839
Check one is meaning: what exactly does each variable measure?

77
00:06:17.839 --> 00:06:20.945
Check two is time: what period does it represent?

78
00:06:20.945 --> 00:06:24.686
Check three is scale: what geography or resolution is used?

79
00:06:24.686 --> 00:06:30.455
Check four is uncertainty: what margin of error, model limitation, or missing data applies?

80
00:06:30.455 --> 00:06:34.702
Check five is comparability: can the layers reasonably be combined?

81
00:06:34.702 --> 00:06:39.901
Check six is ground truth: what field observation or community evidence is needed?

82
00:06:39.901 --> 00:06:44.339
Create two visual versions using different reasonable classifications.

83
00:06:44.339 --> 00:06:46.304
Describe how the story changes.

84
00:06:46.304 --> 00:06:50.425
Do not choose the version that best supports a preferred outcome.

85
00:06:50.425 --> 00:06:56.765
Choose the version that answers the decision question most honestly, and show the underlying values.

86
00:06:56.765 --> 00:06:58.096
Add a privacy screen.

87
00:06:58.096 --> 00:07:04.055
Never publish a map that can identify a vulnerable person or household from sensitive records.

88
00:07:04.055 --> 00:07:06.211
Aggregate or suppress when needed.

89
00:07:06.211 --> 00:07:10.522
Public availability does not automatically make every reuse ethical.

90
00:07:10.522 --> 00:07:12.170
Add a reproducibility log.

91
00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:18.320
Save the query or table identifiers, filters, calculations, software version, and retrieval date.

92
00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:22.187
Give the output a plain-language title and a limitations box.

93
00:07:22.187 --> 00:07:26.688
If you cannot reproduce the number, it is not ready to carry a finding.

94
00:07:26.688 --> 00:07:31.062
Commissioners should ask simple questions that expose complex errors.

95
00:07:31.062 --> 00:07:32.584
What is the denominator?

96
00:07:32.584 --> 00:07:33.725
What year is this?

97
00:07:33.725 --> 00:07:36.578
What does the darkest color mean numerically?

98
00:07:36.578 --> 00:07:39.494
Is the difference larger than the uncertainty?

99
00:07:39.494 --> 00:07:43.425
Are we describing an area or assuming facts about individuals?

100
00:07:43.425 --> 00:07:45.644
What would we see at another scale?

101
00:07:45.644 --> 00:07:46.595
Details change.

102
00:07:46.595 --> 00:07:50.081
A web map can update without a printed packet changing.

103
00:07:50.081 --> 00:07:55.660
Current metadata, source documentation, governing law, and city-attorney advice control.

104
00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.374
Now three recall questions.

105
00:07:57.374 --> 00:07:58.199
Question one.

106
00:07:58.199 --> 00:08:01.499
What should you read before interpreting map colors?

107
00:08:01.499 --> 00:08:01.943
Answer.

108
00:08:01.943 --> 00:08:07.846
The title, purpose, source, date, geography, legend, units, notes, and classification breaks.

109
00:08:07.846 --> 00:08:08.671
Question two.

110
00:08:08.671 --> 00:08:11.844
Why does an ACS estimate need its margin of error?

111
00:08:11.844 --> 00:08:12.288
Answer.

112
00:08:12.288 --> 00:08:14.510
The ACS is a sample-based estimate.

113
00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:21.999
The margin helps show uncertainty and whether apparent differences may be too small to support a confident conclusion.

114
00:08:21.999 --> 00:08:22.951
Question three.

115
00:08:22.951 --> 00:08:25.934
What can Greenprint do, and what can it not do?

116
00:08:25.934 --> 00:08:26.378
Answer.

117
00:08:26.378 --> 00:08:32.407
It can organize public regional layers, reveal overlap, and support questions and alternatives.

118
00:08:32.407 --> 00:08:39.135
It cannot by itself prove causation, parcel conditions, regulatory status, or the correct policy decision.

119
00:08:39.135 --> 00:08:45.608
Your learner artifact is the map-reading card, two classified map versions, and a reproducibility log.

120
00:08:45.608 --> 00:08:48.528
Use only public data you are permitted to use.

121
00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:56.334
Include source, vintage, universe, geography, uncertainty, retrieval date, transformation, privacy review, and limitations.

122
00:08:56.334 --> 00:09:10.918
This lesson draws from SCAG's public Toolbox Tuesday materials from 2023 through 2026 on GIS modeling, Census and ACS analysis, economic statistics, environmental-justice tools, and the September 2026 SoCal Greenprint demonstration.

123
00:09:10.918 --> 00:09:12.427
It also uses public U.S.

124
00:09:12.427 --> 00:09:21.416
Census Bureau guidance on the decennial census, ACS estimates, geographies, and margins of error, plus SCAG's public description of Greenprint.

125
00:09:21.416 --> 00:09:23.050
The narration is original.

126
00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:27.828
No slide, map, recording, source prose, or proprietary data has been copied.

127
00:09:27.828 --> 00:09:35.120
Confirm current metadata, access terms, source documentation, legal requirements, and Torrance city-attorney advice.
